; Show.ax — one value, one String, chosen by the value's static type.
;
; This module is the target the printing macros lower onto. It exists
; so that `(println "n = {n}")` can be a REWRITE: the expander turns
; the hole into `(show n)` and stops there, and everything about
; *how* an `Int` renders is ordinary Axiom code in this file, checked
; and compiled like any other.
;
; WHY A TRAIT AND NOT A BUILT-IN. A hole has to pick a printer, and a
; macro cannot see a type (docs/macro-system.md MAC-CAP-7) - so the
; choice cannot be made at expansion time, and it must not be made at
; RUN time, because that would mean carrying a type tag on every value
; and reading it in the printer. That is reflection, and this language
; does not have it. A trait method is the third option and the only
; one that fits both constraints: the call `(show n)` is emitted
; blind, and the CHECKER resolves it to an instance from the static
; type of `n`, at compile time, into a direct call with no table and
; no indirection (AX3025's note). A hole whose type has no instance
; is a compile error naming the type - which is the property that
; makes format strings type-checked rather than best-effort.
;
; WHY `String`'s INSTANCE IS THE IDENTITY, AND WHY THAT MATTERS. It
; looks like a tautology and it is load-bearing: `(println e)` for a
; non-literal `e` lowers to `(println_ (show e))`, so every call
; written before the printing macros existed - all of which passed an
; already-built `String` - still means exactly what it meant. The
; rename in `IO.ax` cost zero call sites because of this line.
;
; WHAT IS NOT HERE. No `Debug`, no alternate representation, no
; width or precision in the trait: `show` answers the one rendering a
; type has. Padding and rounding are `Fmt`'s functions, applied to the
; result and interpolated - `(println "{padded}")` after
; `(let ((padded (fmtPadLeft (show n) 8))) ...)` - rather than a
; specifier grammar inside the string. That keeps the format-string
; language to holes and braces, which is the whole of what AX3031
; has to explain.

(import Fmt)
(import Str)

(pub trait (Show a) where
  (show :: (-> a String)))

; The four instances the language's own literals produce. A program
; adds its own for its own types, and `stdlib/Pre.ax`'s `deriveShow`
; generates the FUNCTION half for a `data` type - `(deriveShow Color)`
; gives `showColor`, and one line makes it interpolable:
;
;   (impl (Show Color) where ((show (lambda (c) (showColor c)))))
;
(pub impl (Show String) where
  ((show (lambda (s) s))))

(pub impl (Show Int) where
  ((show (lambda (n) (fmtInt n)))))

(pub impl (Show Bool) where
  ((show (lambda (b) (if b "true" "false")))))

; `fmtFloat`'s own default precision, so `{x}` on a Float renders the
; way `fmtFloat` renders and the two cannot drift.
(pub impl (Show Float) where
  ((show (lambda (x) (fmtFloat x)))))

; `format` — a String, built at compile time from a literal's runs and
; holes, or `show` applied to anything else.
;
;   (format "x = {x}, y = {y}")   ; the interpolating form
;   (format n)                    ; = (show n); Rust's format!("{}", n)
;
; The macro is four tokens because `syntax/format` is the whole
; implementation: it reads the literal, splits it, and emits the
; `strConcat` chain with a `show` call per hole. See
; docs/macro-system.md MAC-CAP-10 for the grammar and the lowering.
(pub macro (format e) (syntax/format e))
